From e945a981f03d6fee12be56fcc21442b623432e9b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 08:35:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ci: fail when a clean build changes committed output

ts/dist and ts/dist-test are committed, and nothing checked that they match
what the compiler actually emits. For a long time ts/dist/util.js.map did
not: `make clean && make all` left a dirty tree, so every contributor saw a
phantom diff on a file they had not touched. bd1a2a0 refreshed the map alone
to quiet that, with an empty commit body, and the drift came straight back
because the cause was untouched. 97f992e fixed the artifact; this stops the
class of problem returning unnoticed.

The check is three lines and nearly free: the job has already installed,
built and tested, so this only reads the result. `git status --porcelain`
reports the whole repository even when run from ts/, so no
working-directory override is needed, and a whole-tree check covers
ts/dist-test and any generated file committed later without needing to be
updated. Verified that `npm install` leaves nothing untracked, so the broad
scope does not introduce false failures.

On failure it names the changed files and prints a diffstat, so the fix --
rebuild and commit -- is obvious from the log rather than requiring a local
repro.

Tested both directions, because a check that cannot fail is worse than none:
clean tree exits 0, and with the old stale mapping shape injected back into
ts/dist/util.js.map it exits 1 and names the file. That is the exact defect
it exists to catch.

It runs on both matrix entries. tsc output does not depend on the Node
version, so this should not diverge between 24 and latest; if it ever does,
that is a finding worth having rather than noise.

This is also the other half of pinning typescript exactly in 97f992e. With a
caret range a tsc patch release could change the emitted output and turn
this red with no source change behind it, so the pin is what makes the guard
stable rather than flaky.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01RBcWm7ygX2tNhUtqtTJbAh
---
 .github/workflows/ci.yml | 25 +++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+)

diff --git a/.github/workflows/ci.yml b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
index aee7b8b..ec1751a 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/ci.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
@@ -30,6 +30,31 @@ jobs:
       - run: npm run build
       - run: npm test
 
+      # ts/dist and ts/dist-test are COMMITTED, so a build must not change
+      # them. For a long time ts/dist/util.js.map did not: `make clean && make
+      # all` left a dirty tree, bd1a2a0 refreshed the map on its own rather
+      # than fix it, and the drift came straight back. Nothing caught that,
+      # because nothing compared the committed output against a fresh build.
+      #
+      # The build above already ran, so this only reads the result. `git
+      # status` reports the whole repository even from ts/, which is why no
+      # working-directory override is needed here.
+      #
+      # This is also why typescript is pinned exactly rather than by caret
+      # range: with a range, a tsc patch release could change the emitted
+      # output and turn this red with no source edit behind it.
+      - name: Committed build output must match a clean build
+        run: |
+          set -euo pipefail
+          DIRTY="$(git status --porcelain)"
+          if [ -n "$DIRTY" ]; then
+            echo "::error::a clean build changed committed files; rebuild and commit ts/dist and ts/dist-test"
+            echo "$DIRTY"
+            git --no-pager diff --stat
+            exit 1
+          fi
+          echo "committed build output matches a clean build"
+
   go-build-and-test:
     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
 
-- 
